A||N||3||Cozmo||2.8 oil pan rusted thru!||||8||
Z||000000||Cozmo||11-15-2001||07:10 PM||||Just put a used engine into an auto '86 GT, runs OK but there is a hole rusted through it just below the pulley.  I searched the archives and here's what I think I have to do...<BR>1) get the car up on jackstands high enough to work on it comfortably.<BR>2) remove pass. wheel and liner.<BR>3) drop exhaust and remove starter.<BR>4) remove 2 bolts holding pass. side mount to block.<BR>5) jack up block enough to remove motor mount from cradle.<BR>6) lower block onto some 2x4s placed under crank pully for support.<BR>7) drop oil pan (watch for crank interference, rotate crank if needed).<BR>8) get rubber gasket, RTV it to new pan and bolt it back on.<BR>9) reassembly is simply a reverse of dissasembly (Yeah right...)<BR>I understand you can notch the motor mount to provide clearance so you don't have to remove it. The car is an auto, does all the above still apply?<BR>Is there anything else I should take care of while I'm in there? (the donor engine has 85K on it). Any suggestions or tricks will be greatly appreciated!<P>Thanks,<P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroRacer||11-16-2001||05:05 PM||||While you're up there, you may want to just run a torque wrench over the bearing cap bolts just for safe practices.  Clean or replace the oil pump screen, maybe even just change rod bearings (They're cheap and easy).  I had to open my 85 gt auto up for repairs so I have a few more things I'll have to do.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||11-16-2001||06:04 PM||||clean up like said above. but if you dont have any problems, leave stuff like bearings alone. Remember the old saying 'if it aint broke, dont fix it'. I dont want to be the one a month down the road saying 'told ya so.'||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||11-16-2001||07:46 PM||||What Roger said. <BR>If it's not making any funny noises, don't replace the bearings. It's painstaking enough to get the clearances right with a clean engine on a stand. Never mind when you're on your back with grit falling in your eyes. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
